n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4(-8)y.\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=-8.$ Therefore, our parabola has its vertex at $(0,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-efa5ab19-180d-5954-8556-a06af878d726"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-efa5ab19-180d-5954-8556-a06af878d726" :answer-field/id #uuid "efa5ab19-180d-5954-8556-a06af878d726" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dd3a08ea-21fd-5000-b8a1-a944e3f82dcf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9a9962f6-4409-54cc-9ffe-bac51b895587" "answer-406c2ea3-2d53-54d0-8b54-ee986e38b7e0" "answer-dd3a08ea-21fd-5000-b8a1-a944e3f82dcf" "answer-21e21adf-8388-5eb9-9be5-b1e66c6a42be" "answer-b6636b71-c496-5f64-9118-ecd49e20ef49"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a9962f6-4409-54cc-9ffe-bac51b895587" :answer/id #uuid "9a9962f6-4409-54cc-9ffe-bac51b895587" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,-32)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-406c2ea3-2d53-54d0-8b54-ee986e38b7e0" :answer/id #uuid "406c2ea3-2d53-54d0-8b54-ee986e38b7e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,-8)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd3a08ea-21fd-5000-b8a1-a944e3f82dcf" :answer/id #uuid "dd3a08ea-21fd-5000-b8a1-a944e3f82dcf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-21e21adf-8388-5eb9-9be5-b1e66c6a42be" :answer/id #uuid "21e21adf-8388-5eb9-9be5-b1e66c6a42be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(32,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6636b71-c496-5f64-9118-ecd49e20ef49" :answer/id #uuid "b6636b71-c496-5f64-9118-ecd49e20ef49" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(8,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e7d54a09-b694-5b89-8845-bc7803f20047" :question/id #uuid "e7d54a09-b694-5b89-8845-bc7803f20047" :question/math-academy-id "q-115899" :question/problem "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${x}^{2}=8y?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at the origin is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4(2)y.\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=2.$ Therefore, our parabola has its vertex at $(0,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-534da900-1fbe-51e4-99fb-8533ecb549b3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-534da900-1fbe-51e4-99fb-8533ecb549b3" :answer-field/id #uuid "534da900-1fbe-51e4-99fb-8533ecb549b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4d9d96ab-7956-54d4-b87d-c67431ae3cdd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4d9d96ab-7956-54d4-b87d-c67431ae3cdd" "answer-25577b79-20d0-5f6d-ac86-3c6642a884ef" "answer-5cca508d-ea68-5f55-8f2d-867e6ecf6d51" "answer-2a1d2d6a-4812-5cc2-8283-c1f0849d4a82" "answer-1263c9fd-316a-54af-888d-1482c4f0103f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d9d96ab-7956-54d4-b87d-c67431ae3cdd" :answer/id #uuid "4d9d96ab-7956-54d4-b87d-c67431ae3cdd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25577b79-20d0-5f6d-ac86-3c6642a884ef" :answer/id #uuid "25577b79-20d0-5f6d-ac86-3c6642a884ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5cca508d-ea68-5f55-8f2d-867e6ecf6d51" :answer/id #uuid "5cca508d-ea68-5f55-8f2d-867e6ecf6d51" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,8)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a1d2d6a-4812-5cc2-8283-c1f0849d4a82" :answer/id #uuid "2a1d2d6a-4812-5cc2-8283-c1f0849d4a82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1263c9fd-316a-54af-888d-1482c4f0103f" :answer/id #uuid "1263c9fd-316a-54af-888d-1482c4f0103f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(8,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096763 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b967fd96-33d4-527f-b0fa-13d15459432d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-b967fd96-33d4-527f-b0fa-13d15459432d" :question/id #uuid "b967fd96-33d4-527f-b0fa-13d15459432d" :question/math-academy-id "q-84973" :question/problem "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $(y-2)^{2}=24x?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n(y-2)^{2}=4(6)(x-0).\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=6.$ \n\nComparing our equation with the standard equation, we can see that we have $h=0$ and $k=2.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola are $(0,2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e5c471f1-7322-5546-9274-391fcdaf58fb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e5c471f1-7322-5546-9274-391fcdaf58fb" :answer-field/id #uuid "e5c471f1-7322-5546-9274-391fcdaf58fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b173c4e6-d3a3-5998-84d4-1bcdb40cef0e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6e2d5e63-bd1a-5877-af7c-ee76ddd010f0" "answer-981388fd-f53b-5c00-b3df-ab2e5c3b2ce6" "answer-b173c4e6-d3a3-5998-84d4-1bcdb40cef0e" "answer-52e0fa5e-82bb-5d66-89a7-3e3c3038f2fd" "answer-f0551018-f5de-55f6-9abc-7cd335232b8d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e2d5e63-bd1a-5877-af7c-ee76ddd010f0" :answer/id #uuid "6e2d5e63-bd1a-5877-af7c-ee76ddd010f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2,6)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-981388fd-f53b-5c00-b3df-ab2e5c3b2ce6" :answer/id #uuid "981388fd-f53b-5c00-b3df-ab2e5c3b2ce6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b173c4e6-d3a3-5998-84d4-1bcdb40cef0e" :answer/id #uuid "b173c4e6-d3a3-5998-84d4-1bcdb40cef0e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52e0fa5e-82bb-5d66-89a7-3e3c3038f2fd" :answer/id #uuid "52e0fa5e-82bb-5d66-89a7-3e3c3038f2fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f0551018-f5de-55f6-9abc-7cd335232b8d" :answer/id #uuid "f0551018-f5de-55f6-9abc-7cd335232b8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(6,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119952 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e5baa90b-efb9-5b0e-bee8-5a62fcaafd24"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-e5baa90b-efb9-5b0e-bee8-5a62fcaafd24" :question/id #uuid "e5baa90b-efb9-5b0e-bee8-5a62fcaafd24" :question/math-academy-id "q-67498" :question/problem "What is the $5$th term of the geometric sequence given below?\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=-\\frac{1}{2}f(n),\\,f(1)=80,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the recursive formula to compute the terms of the sequence up to the $5$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =80 \\\\ f(2) & =-\\frac{1}{2}f(1)=-\\frac{1}{2}(80)=-40 \\\\ f(3) & =-\\frac{1}{2}f(2)=-\\frac{1}{2}(-40)=20 \\\\ f(4) & =-\\frac{1}{2}f(3)=-\\frac{1}{2}(20)=-10 \\\\ f(5) & =-\\frac{1}{2}f(4)=-\\frac{1}{2}(-10)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(5)=5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8f97bdd2-bf1f-57d2-a993-56bdac1ca568"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8f97bdd2-bf1f-57d2-a993-56bdac1ca568" :answer-field/id #uuid "8f97bdd2-bf1f-57d2-a993-56bdac1ca568" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ffb6401b-1262-5f0b-b7c9-aea70518da43" :answer-field/choices ["answer-18edd661-2027-5e28-96f0-f828f0dff38b" "answer-3b0ddaba-2c82-500c-808e-1d839bf00de9" "answer-5e33967c-829f-5beb-b8b0-ea9386b9050d" "answer-9f6ee3a8-a1e6-5268-9d51-b43dc805f1e8" "answer-ffb6401b-1262-5f0b-b7c9-aea70518da43"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18edd661-2027-5e28-96f0-f828f0dff38b" :answer/id #uuid "18edd661-2027-5e28-96f0-f828f0dff38b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b0ddaba-2c82-500c-808e-1d839bf00de9" :answer/id #uuid "3b0ddaba-2c82-500c-808e-1d839bf00de9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e33967c-829f-5beb-b8b0-ea9386b9050d" :answer/id #uuid "5e33967c-829f-5beb-b8b0-ea9386b9050d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f6ee3a8-a1e6-5268-9d51-b43dc805f1e8" :answer/id #uuid "9f6ee3a8-a1e6-5268-9d51-b43dc805f1e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ffb6401b-1262-5f0b-b7c9-aea70518da43" :answer/id #uuid "ffb6401b-1262-5f0b-b7c9-aea70518da43" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119953 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-42a89ddb-b42b-5afd-9218-01f794cbc72e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-42a89ddb-b42b-5afd-9218-01f794cbc72e" :question/id #uuid "42a89ddb-b42b-5afd-9218-01f794cbc72e" :question/math-academy-id "q-67501" :question/problem "Which of the three sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,2,\\,…$\n\nII. ${a}_{n+1}=\\frac{3}{2}{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=-1,\\,n\\ge 1$\n\nIII. $1,\\,0.2,\\,0.03,\\,…$" :question/worked-solution "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I is not a geometric sequence because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{4})} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is a geometric sequence. The recursive rule ${a}_{n+1}=\\frac{3}{2}{a}_{n}$ tells us that each term is $\\frac{3}{2}$ times the previous term, so the common ratio is $r=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n- Sequence III is not a geometric sequence because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{0.2}{1} & =0.2 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{0.03}{0.2} & =0.15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only sequence II is a geometric sequence." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0d1259d7-7153-5c05-8443-bfcd08dbb965"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0d1259d7-7153-5c05-8443-bfcd08dbb965" :answer-field/id #uuid "0d1259d7-7153-5c05-8443-bfcd08dbb965" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ccbdc6ea-8277-599f-9f2d-8846ff5d253b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6aeed908-a161-5da2-adf1-5808f16f101a" "answer-71bfc73a-a14f-5c8b-a23c-4a17d56fd53e" "answer-3a977656-5bc0-52c2-8412-7159dca353b5" "answer-ccbdc6ea-8277-599f-9f2d-8846ff5d253b" "answer-4727a762-fdee-5b8f-ab0a-5ac69d4cf5e3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6aeed908-a161-5da2-adf1-5808f16f101a" :answer/id #uuid "6aeed908-a161-5da2-adf1-5808f16f101a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71bfc73a-a14f-5c8b-a23c-4a17d56fd53e" :answer/id #uuid "71bfc73a-a14f-5c8b-a23c-4a17d56fd53e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a977656-5bc0-52c2-8412-7159dca353b5" :answer/id #uuid "3a977656-5bc0-52c2-8412-7159dca353b5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ccbdc6ea-8277-599f-9f2d-8846ff5d253b" :answer/id #uuid "ccbdc6ea-8277-599f-9f2d-8846ff5d253b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4727a762-fdee-5b8f-ab0a-5ac69d4cf5e3" :answer/id #uuid "4727a762-fdee-5b8f-ab0a-5ac69d4cf5e3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119951 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-857b63a1-09cd-5ba9-9404-4c37877c9864"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-857b63a1-09cd-5ba9-9404-4c37877c9864" :question/id #uuid "857b63a1-09cd-5ba9-9404-4c37877c9864" :question/math-academy-id "q-79511" :question/problem "What is the $4$th term of the geometric sequence given below?\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=-6{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{36},\\,n\\ge 1\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the recursive formula to compute the terms of the sequence up to the $4$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{36} \\\\ {a}_{2} & =-6{a}_{1}=-6(\\frac{1}{36})=-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =-6{a}_{2}=-6(-\\frac{1}{6})=1 \\\\ {a}_{4} & =-6{a}_{3}=-6(1)=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${a}_{4}=-6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e8f8080f-0b51-545f-9d71-93e2c857b6d4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e8f8080f-0b51-545f-9d71-93e2c857b6d4" :answer-field/id #uuid "e8f8080f-0b51-545f-9d71-93e2c857b6d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9ba3a52b-1145-5782-88c9-4be78cb79a7e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-64bcca0d-1370-5e1e-9384-b9fe6e5c2226" "answer-9ba3a52b-1145-5782-88c9-4be78cb79a7e" "answer-53773b9d-b41d-57fa-9993-8ee73ebf788b" "answer-74903b10-b9c3-5982-a77c-38ecc70f9cf4" "answer-e4bf7c3d-ba90-54f4-a876-93efb8156cca"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-64bcca0d-1370-5e1e-9384-b9fe6e5c2226" :answer/id #uuid "64bcca0d-1370-5e1e-9384-b9fe6e5c2226" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ba3a52b-1145-5782-88c9-4be78cb79a7e" :answer/id #uuid "9ba3a52b-1145-5782-88c9-4be78cb79a7e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-53773b9d-b41d-57fa-9993-8ee73ebf788b" :answer/id #uuid "53773b9d-b41d-57fa-9993-8ee73ebf788b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-74903b10-b9c3-5982-a77c-38ecc70f9cf4" :answer/id #uuid "74903b10-b9c3-5982-a77c-38ecc70f9cf4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4bf7c3d-ba90-54f4-a876-93efb8156cca" :answer/id #uuid "e4bf7c3d-ba90-54f4-a876-93efb8156cca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-ded1d378-db86-565b-8eaa-f9725c21c11b" :activity/id #uuid "ded1d378-db86-565b-8eaa-f9725c21c11b" :activity/title "The Recursive Formula for a Geometric Sequence" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048829 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-6eaf6155-fd31-5612-a495-df002400eda9" "step-90cd1598-b2f8-5d28-8110-090cd43a789d" "step-d7c48d56-9ae9-568b-9b4a-0e104f0a6f6d"] :activity/first-step "step-6eaf6155-fd31-5612-a495-df002400eda9" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-6eaf6155-fd31-5612-a495-df002400eda9" :step/id #uuid "6eaf6155-fd31-5612-a495-df002400eda9" :step/content "question-e5baa90b-efb9-5b0e-bee8-5a62fcaafd24" :step/next "step-90cd1598-b2f8-5d28-8110-090cd43a789d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-90cd1598-b2f8-5d28-8110-090cd43a789d" :step/id #uuid "90cd1598-b2f8-5d28-8110-090cd43a789d" :step/content "question-42a89ddb-b42b-5afd-9218-01f794cbc72e" :step/next "step-d7c48d56-9ae9-568b-9b4a-0e104f0a6f6d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d7c48d56-9ae9-568b-9b4a-0e104f0a6f6d" :step/id #uuid "d7c48d56-9ae9-568b-9b4a-0e104f0a6f6d" :step/content "question-857b63a1-09cd-5ba9-9404-4c37877c9864" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124466 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-244be0b4-aca1-5c1c-abe2-94099e0fbbe5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-244be0b4-aca1-5c1c-abe2-94099e0fbbe5" :question/id #uuid "244be0b4-aca1-5c1c-abe2-94099e0fbbe5" :question/math-academy-id "q-132751" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{z+y-{x}^{2}}?$\n\nI. The partial derivative with respect to $x$ is defined at $(0,0,1)$\n\nII. ${f}_{y}(1,2,0)$ is defined\n\nIII. ${f}_{z}(1,1,0)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{z+y-{x}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z+y-{x}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z+y-{x}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(0,0,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,0,1)=\\sqrt{1+0-{0}^{2}}=1,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(0,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,0,1) & =-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{1+0-{0}^{2}}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,2,0)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,2,0)=\\sqrt{0+2-{1}^{2}}=1,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(1,2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,2,0) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{0+2-{1}^{2}}}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(1,1,0)$ into ${f}_{z}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{z}(1,1,0)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{0+1-{1}^{2}}}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only'." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-73207714-b850-5ffd-8c57-6fa10d88caae"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-73207714-b850-5ffd-8c57-6fa10d88caae" :answer-field/id #uuid "73207714-b850-5ffd-8c57-6fa10d88caae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6b1e694a-1db7-5721-b7ce-8435d79a5611" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6b1e694a-1db7-5721-b7ce-8435d79a5611" "answer-dcde3ca7-4fbb-5c0b-bafc-57f55ce518b8" "answer-dfc647e1-2b5b-579f-92cf-20efc3da4346" "answer-8fd0ef33-4fca-54c2-829e-50a7815c63c8" "answer-faf5e9ca-69be-5548-8107-7d06c638adcc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b1e694a-1db7-5721-b7ce-8435d79a5611" :answer/id #uuid "6b1e694a-1db7-5721-b7ce-8435d79a5611" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dcde3ca7-4fbb-5c0b-bafc-57f55ce518b8" :answer/id #uuid "dcde3ca7-4fbb-5c0b-bafc-57f55ce518b8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dfc647e1-2b5b-579f-92cf-20efc3da4346" :answer/id #uuid "dfc647e1-2b5b-579f-92cf-20efc3da4346" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8fd0ef33-4fca-54c2-829e-50a7815c63c8" :answer/id #uuid "8fd0ef33-4fca-54c2-829e-50a7815c63c8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-faf5e9ca-69be-5548-8107-7d06c638adcc" :answer/id #uuid "faf5e9ca-69be-5548-8107-7d06c638adcc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-062eec2e-80dd-50ca-8380-634dda28eed3" :activity/id #uuid "062eec2e-80dd-50ca-8380-634dda28eed3" :activity/title "Partial Differentiability of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048877 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-2842369f-1f4f-58be-953d-3f713b247f9b" "step-6bba2763-669c-58c3-a3e6-5843dc68f94f" "step-ad4fbff0-775d-5cc6-a06c-c04820df7660" "step-a90d8bfb-8461-5668-8e11-2111cb68de7c" "step-33af0b6f-ae95-578f-99a9-1e3131763eb2"] :activity/first-step "step-2842369f-1f4f-58be-953d-3f713b247f9b" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-2842369f-1f4f-58be-953d-3f713b247f9b" :step/id #uuid "2842369f-1f4f-58be-953d-3f713b247f9b" :step/content 17592186323840 :step/next "step-6bba2763-669c-58c3-a3e6-5843dc68f94f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6bba2763-669c-58c3-a3e6-5843dc68f94f" :step/id #uuid "6bba2763-669c-58c3-a3e6-5843dc68f94f" :step/content 17592186125193 :step/next "step-ad4fbff0-775d-5cc6-a06c-c04820df7660" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ad4fbff0-775d-5cc6-a06c-c04820df7660" :step/id #uuid "ad4fbff0-775d-5cc6-a06c-c04820df7660" :step/content 17592186339041 :step/next "step-a90d8bfb-8461-5668-8e11-2111cb68de7c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a90d8bfb-8461-5668-8e11-2111cb68de7c" :step/id #uuid "a90d8bfb-8461-5668-8e11-2111cb68de7c" :step/content 17592186381041 :step/next "step-33af0b6f-ae95-578f-99a9-1e3131763eb2" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-33af0b6f-ae95-578f-99a9-1e3131763eb2" :step/id #uuid "33af0b6f-ae95-578f-99a9-1e3131763eb2" :step/content "question-244be0b4-aca1-5c1c-abe2-94099e0fbbe5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157437 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-12b67edd-e409-5ea0-9859-9fa94b363ebb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-12b67edd-e409-5ea0-9859-9fa94b363ebb" :question/id #uuid "12b67edd-e409-5ea0-9859-9fa94b363ebb" :question/math-academy-id "q-88457" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}{e}^{3x}-4x{e}^{3x}-12{e}^{3x}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}-4x{e}^{3x}-12{e}^{3x} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-4x-12){e}^{3x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-4x-12=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{e}^{3x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-4x-12=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+2)(x-6)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-2,6.$\n\n- The equation ${e}^{3x}=0$ has no solutions because ${e}^{3x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-2,6,$ and their product is $(-2)\\cdot 6=-12.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7de9c953-9396-5e7b-9be9-017863373246"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7de9c953-9396-5e7b-9be9-017863373246" :answer-field/id #uuid "7de9c953-9396-5e7b-9be9-017863373246" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-660eda51-d232-5b88-9bd1-a2c03b96321a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-660eda51-d232-5b88-9bd1-a2c03b96321a" "answer-61b10d9f-a72c-52f5-9148-9af198ae4115" "answer-44378ce2-6b6d-50f9-8219-f673e5079ada" "answer-cb9d3203-fb35-582c-af0f-5edb1642fe68" "answer-605ed3c1-ae12-54a3-81f7-60de52284930"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-660eda51-d232-5b88-9bd1-a2c03b96321a" :answer/id #uuid "660eda51-d232-5b88-9bd1-a2c03b96321a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-61b10d9f-a72c-52f5-9148-9af198ae4115" :answer/id #uuid "61b10d9f-a72c-52f5-9148-9af198ae4115" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44378ce2-6b6d-50f9-8219-f673e5079ada" :answer/id #uuid "44378ce2-6b6d-50f9-8219-f673e5079ada" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cb9d3203-fb35-582c-af0f-5edb1642fe68" :answer/id #uuid "cb9d3203-fb35-582c-af0f-5edb1642fe68" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-605ed3c1-ae12-54a3-81f7-60de52284930" :answer/id #uuid "605ed3c1-ae12-54a3-81f7-60de52284930" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157438 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e2c0ab81-ce73-5f44-b936-e8bb7ee9594f" "question-3499241b-bbd1-5943-8b7b-1fe387959bfa"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-e2c0ab81-ce73-5f44-b936-e8bb7ee9594f" :question/id #uuid "e2c0ab81-ce73-5f44-b936-e8bb7ee9594f" :question/math-academy-id "q-100794" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $3{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x}=11x\\cdot {5}^{x}+4\\cdot {5}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x} & =11x\\cdot {5}^{x}+4\\cdot {5}^{x} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x}-11x\\cdot {5}^{x}-4\\cdot {5}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (3{x}^{2}-11x-4)\\cdot {5}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}-11x-4=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $3{x}^{2}-11x-4=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(3x+1)(x-4)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\frac{1}{3},4.$\n\n- The equation ${5}^{x}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-\\frac{1}{3},4,$ and their product is $(-\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot 4=-\\frac{4}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dc076be1-fa1d-5a8e-9f7b-f0d944a99952"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dc076be1-fa1d-5a8e-9f7b-f0d944a99952" :answer-field/id #uuid "dc076be1-fa1d-5a8e-9f7b-f0d944a99952" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d952e124-7588-56a7-81b3-154bceef8a55" :answer-field/choices ["answer-98428f30-5194-58f2-b856-5fc56747aac9" "answer-d6757daa-1d29-5c10-947c-afd176f4f5d9" "answer-6ea8cba7-d03a-516f-a0c3-30fb8354dfdd" "answer-86253246-2caa-5e0b-abae-547f561590f3" "answer-d952e124-7588-56a7-81b3-154bceef8a55"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98428f30-5194-58f2-b856-5fc56747aac9" :answer/id #uuid "98428f30-5194-58f2-b856-5fc56747aac9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6757daa-1d29-5c10-947c-afd176f4f5d9" :answer/id #uuid "d6757daa-1d29-5c10-947c-afd176f4f5d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ea8cba7-d03a-516f-a0c3-30fb8354dfdd" :answer/id #uuid "6ea8cba7-d03a-516f-a0c3-30fb8354dfdd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-86253246-2caa-5e0b-abae-547f561590f3" :answer/id #uuid "86253246-2caa-5e0b-abae-547f561590f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d952e124-7588-56a7-81b3-154bceef8a55" :answer/id #uuid "d952e124-7588-56a7-81b3-154bceef8a55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3499241b-bbd1-5943-8b7b-1fe387959bfa" :question/id #uuid "3499241b-bbd1-5943-8b7b-1fe387959bfa" :question/math-academy-id "q-100799" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $9{x}^{2}{e}^{x}=36{e}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}{e}^{x} & =36{e}^{x} \\\\ 9{x}^{2}{e}^{x}-36{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (9{x}^{2}-36){e}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}-36=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{e}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $9{x}^{2}-36=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(3x+6)(3x-6)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-2,2.$\n\n- The equation ${e}^{x}=0$ has no solutions because ${e}^{x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-2,2,$ and their product is $(-2)\\cdot 2=-4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-459604d3-c555-5e7f-b4d3-0ad596a161eb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-459604d3-c555-5e7f-b4d3-0ad596a161eb" :answer-field/id #uuid "459604d3-c555-5e7f-b4d3-0ad596a161eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ea325e7e-7c0b-5864-bded-5700ce421acf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4c7135a8-799d-5ef5-9d3d-470881e76d26" "answer-ea325e7e-7c0b-5864-bded-5700ce421acf" "answer-adcaa9ff-45f7-5db6-b2e3-d82e71396783" "answer-d2eb82f2-a647-5584-afd7-3c3b5045b584" "answer-beb695a1-1361-556b-b4fb-bedb79dc1f93"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c7135a8-799d-5ef5-9d3d-470881e76d26" :answer/id #uuid "4c7135a8-799d-5ef5-9d3d-470881e76d26" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea325e7e-7c0b-5864-bded-5700ce421acf" :answer/id #uuid "ea325e7e-7c0b-5864-bded-5700ce421acf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-adcaa9ff-45f7-5db6-b2e3-d82e71396783" :answer/id #uuid "adcaa9ff-45f7-5db6-b2e3-d82e71396783" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2eb82f2-a647-5584-afd7-3c3b5045b584" :answer/id #uuid "d2eb82f2-a647-5584-afd7-3c3b5045b584" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-beb695a1-1361-556b-b4fb-bedb79dc1f93" :answer/id #uuid "beb695a1-1361-556b-b4fb-bedb79dc1f93" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-dc729c17-5f64-5682-98ca-64ec703a1464" :activity/id #uuid "dc