id #uuid "29efc143-8ef6-514b-b08b-b09cf3c6dccf" :question/math-academy-id "q-64861" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "The angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d78da839-c447-5f6a-b9f4-52fee3e7c783"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d78da839-c447-5f6a-b9f4-52fee3e7c783" :answer-field/id #uuid "d78da839-c447-5f6a-b9f4-52fee3e7c783" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6238dcb6-e730-5761-a8fc-314e6a8b5732" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bc748a1d-aa55-53b1-8727-38e8aeaf186e" "answer-5e88955e-5bc1-5000-8512-3eeafc61d0f6" "answer-6238dcb6-e730-5761-a8fc-314e6a8b5732" "answer-751d8865-5818-58ec-a5f2-b7595be506a7" "answer-a54b68c4-6f83-5b6d-a8d5-c61dfd48387e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc748a1d-aa55-53b1-8727-38e8aeaf186e" :answer/id #uuid "bc748a1d-aa55-53b1-8727-38e8aeaf186e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e88955e-5bc1-5000-8512-3eeafc61d0f6" :answer/id #uuid "5e88955e-5bc1-5000-8512-3eeafc61d0f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6238dcb6-e730-5761-a8fc-314e6a8b5732" :answer/id #uuid "6238dcb6-e730-5761-a8fc-314e6a8b5732" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-751d8865-5818-58ec-a5f2-b7595be506a7" :answer/id #uuid "751d8865-5818-58ec-a5f2-b7595be506a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a54b68c4-6f83-5b6d-a8d5-c61dfd48387e" :answer/id #uuid "a54b68c4-6f83-5b6d-a8d5-c61dfd48387e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Undefined" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186060256 :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186060338 :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059842 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-621a7d94-aa15-5da2-a86e-6e37bead83f1" "question-5dc0c374-75b0-5038-a236-6349d3712634"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-621a7d94-aa15-5da2-a86e-6e37bead83f1" :question/id #uuid "621a7d94-aa15-5da2-a86e-6e37bead83f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-123585" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡({180}^{∘})+\\operatorname{csc}⁡({90}^{∘}).$" :question/worked-solution "The angles ${180}^{∘}$ and ${90}^{∘}$ are quadrantal angles. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\n- Let's first consider the angle ${180}^{∘}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle ${180}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-1,0).$  Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡({180}^{∘})=-1.$ Therefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡({180}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡({180}^{∘})}=\\frac{1}{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\n- Now, let's consider the angle ${90}^{∘}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle ${90}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$  Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({90}^{∘})=1.$ Therefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡({90}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({90}^{∘})}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡({180}^{∘})+\\operatorname{csc}⁡({90}^{∘})=-1+1=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-99de1166-6614-59dc-ba88-9b3adbcbf5e8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-99de1166-6614-59dc-ba88-9b3adbcbf5e8" :answer-field/id #uuid "99de1166-6614-59dc-ba88-9b3adbcbf5e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-16e33752-dbec-5be0-abda-0b23cc352083" :answer-field/choices ["answer-65a2005c-6f9c-5b3b-92b5-b91a280aa805" "answer-7ac225ab-a108-5cf1-b663-e25ab4138d7b" "answer-16e33752-dbec-5be0-abda-0b23cc352083" "answer-fc8fae09-f797-5025-b37c-64365f7ed7cd" "answer-80712ae4-c0c2-56af-a31a-939b00f59f46"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65a2005c-6f9c-5b3b-92b5-b91a280aa805" :answer/id #uuid "65a2005c-6f9c-5b3b-92b5-b91a280aa805" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ac225ab-a108-5cf1-b663-e25ab4138d7b" :answer/id #uuid "7ac225ab-a108-5cf1-b663-e25ab4138d7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16e33752-dbec-5be0-abda-0b23cc352083" :answer/id #uuid "16e33752-dbec-5be0-abda-0b23cc352083" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc8fae09-f797-5025-b37c-64365f7ed7cd" :answer/id #uuid "fc8fae09-f797-5025-b37c-64365f7ed7cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80712ae4-c0c2-56af-a31a-939b00f59f46" :answer/id #uuid "80712ae4-c0c2-56af-a31a-939b00f59f46" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Undefined" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5dc0c374-75b0-5038-a236-6349d3712634" :question/id #uuid "5dc0c374-75b0-5038-a236-6349d3712634" :question/math-academy-id "q-23418" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "The angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{1}{0}.\n$$\n\nHowever, division by zero is undefined. Therefore, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is undefined." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2ba2b05c-984d-5188-ba82-e7eff754bb83"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2ba2b05c-984d-5188-ba82-e7eff754bb83" :answer-field/id #uuid "2ba2b05c-984d-5188-ba82-e7eff754bb83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-08429320-9580-541b-a6d9-31a45aa346b8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b00a2584-97c8-50c5-81c4-5f4529530f60" "answer-42d752c4-1ced-5442-9d14-2e028d4482e2" "answer-a04922be-e134-5966-b10b-e756217fb61e" "answer-5b0f6439-02a9-5ffc-b967-796d14fd99c9" "answer-08429320-9580-541b-a6d9-31a45aa346b8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b00a2584-97c8-50c5-81c4-5f4529530f60" :answer/id #uuid "b00a2584-97c8-50c5-81c4-5f4529530f60" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42d752c4-1ced-5442-9d14-2e028d4482e2" :answer/id #uuid "42d752c4-1ced-5442-9d14-2e028d4482e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a04922be-e134-5966-b10b-e756217fb61e" :answer/id #uuid "a04922be-e134-5966-b10b-e756217fb61e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b0f6439-02a9-5ffc-b967-796d14fd99c9" :answer/id #uuid "5b0f6439-02a9-5ffc-b967-796d14fd99c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-08429320-9580-541b-a6d9-31a45aa346b8" :answer/id #uuid "08429320-9580-541b-a6d9-31a45aa346b8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Undefined" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186060905 :tutorial/content "A **quadrantal angle** is an angle whose terminal side lies on the $x$ or $y$-axis. \nThe unit circle below illustrates the quadrantal angles that lie on the range $[0^\\circ, 360^\\circ).$\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nWe can identify four points on the unit circle above,\n\n$$\n(1,0), \\quad (0,1), \\quad (-1,0), \\quad (0,-1),\n$$\n\nthat correspond to the angles\n\n$$\n0^\\circ, \\quad 90^\\circ,\\quad 180^\\circ, \\quad 270^\\circ,\n$$\n\nin degrees, or\n\n$$\n0, \\quad \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\quad \\pi, \\quad \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},\n$$\n\nin radians.\n\nWe can use these points to find cosine and sine of quadrantal angles. First, recall that a point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nFor example, if we are asked to find the value of $\\cos(\\pi),$ then we just look at the $\\color{blue}x$-coordinate of the corresponding point. The point is $({\\color{blue}{-1}},0),$ and therefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\cos(\\pi)={\\color{blue}{-1}}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059944 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204024 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-272bf8c6-2369-5e98-9adb-ebe6db7bece2" "question-cd16fbf2-f5ff-5242-b610-fb61c79ec2b3" "question-08790f4f-fd5e-5556-9203-49eaf6e8edd8" "question-5830491e-19ce-53b6-8fb0-b5e123e740a5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-272bf8c6-2369-5e98-9adb-ebe6db7bece2" :question/id #uuid "272bf8c6-2369-5e98-9adb-ebe6db7bece2" :question/math-academy-id "q-257192" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-2,2]\\times [0,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-xy-2x.\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[-1,\\,0]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$0.348$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-xy-2x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-xy-2x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4x{y}^{2}+6x-y-2 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}y-x\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x{y}^{2}+6x-y-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{2}y-x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x{y}^{2}+6x-y-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}y-x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4{y}^{2}+6 & 8xy-1 \\\\ 8xy-1 & 4{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{\\begin{bmatrix}4{y}^{2}+6 & 8xy-1 \\\\ 8xy-1 & 4{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}4x{y}^{2}+6x-y-2 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}y-x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with\n${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{\\begin{bmatrix}4{y}^{2}+6 & 8xy-1 \\\\ 8xy-1 & 4{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}4x{y}^{2}+6x-y-2 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}y-x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}4(0)^{2}+6 & 8(-1)(0)-1 \\\\ 8(-1)(0)-1 & 4(-1)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}4(-1)(0)^{2}+6(-1)-0-2 \\\\ 4(-1)^{2}(0)-(-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{6\\cdot 4-(-1)\\cdot (-1)}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{23}\\begin{bmatrix}-31 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}0.348 \\\\ 0.087\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9ae66afd-66cd-529a-9898-9254316b1674"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9ae66afd-66cd-529a-9898-9254316b1674" :answer-field/id #uuid "9ae66afd-66cd-529a-9898-9254316b1674" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-fae98102-ed44-578d-91b3-534204bfeae8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fae98102-ed44-578d-91b3-534204bfeae8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fae98102-ed44-578d-91b3-534204bfeae8" :answer/id #uuid "fae98102-ed44-578d-91b3-534204bfeae8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.087" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cd16fbf2-f5ff-5242-b610-fb61c79ec2b3" :question/id #uuid "cd16fbf2-f5ff-5242-b610-fb61c79ec2b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-257191" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-2,0]\\times [-2,0]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=4{x}^{2}y+3{x}^{2}-xy-2x.\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[-1,\\,-1]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$-0.444$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{2}y+3{x}^{2}-xy-2x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}y+3{x}^{2}-xy-2x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8xy+6x-y-2 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-x\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8xy+6x-y-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{2}-x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(8xy+6x-y-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}-x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8y+6 & 8x-1 \\\\ 8x-1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{\\begin{bmatrix}8y+6 & 8x-1 \\\\ 8x-1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}8xy+6x-y-2 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with\n${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{\\begin{bmatrix}8y+6 & 8x-1 \\\\ 8x-1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}8xy+6x-y-2 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}8(-1)+6 & 8(-1)-1 \\\\ 8(-1)-1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}8(-1)(-1)+6(-1)-(-1)-2 \\\\ 4(-1)^{2}-(-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot 0-(-9)\\cdot (-9)}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 9 \\\\ 9 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{81}\\begin{bmatrix}45 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}-0.444 \\\\ -1.012\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b1b8b6be-a31c-573e-bf6e-e966d1666af4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b1b8b6be-a31c-573e-bf6e-e966d1666af4" :answer-field/id #uuid "b1b8b6be-a31c-573e-bf6e-e966d1666af4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-0f6eed4d-3207-5871-b732-3aaed9f76d38" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0f6eed4d-3207-5871-b732-3aaed9f76d38"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f6eed4d-3207-5871-b732-3aaed9f76d38" :answer/id #uuid "0f6eed4d-3207-5871-b732-3aaed9f76d38" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1.012" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-08790f4f-fd5e-5556-9203-49eaf6e8edd8" :question/id #uuid "08790f4f-fd5e-5556-9203-49eaf6e8edd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-257195" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-1,1]\\times [-1,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y+3{x}^{2}-3xy+2x-{y}^{2}-5.\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[-1,\\,-1]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}y+3{x}^{2}-3xy+2x-{y}^{2}-5) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}y+3{x}^{2}-3xy+2x-{y}^{2}-5)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4xy+6x-3y+2 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-3x-2y\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy+6x-3y+2) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-3x-2y) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy+6x-3y+2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-3x-2y)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4y+6 & 4x-3 \\\\ 4x-3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{\\begin{bmatrix}4y+6 & 4x-3 \\\\ 4x-3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}4xy+6x-3y+2 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-3x-2y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with\n${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{\\begin{bmatrix}4y+6 & 4x-3 \\\\ 4x-3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}4xy+6x-3y+2 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-3x-2y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}4(-1)+6 & 4(-1)-3 \\\\ 4(-1)-3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}4(-1)(-1)+6(-1)-3(-1)+2 \\\\ 2(-1)^{2}-3(-1)-2(-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ -7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (-2)-(-7)\\cdot (-7)}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{53}\\begin{bmatrix}43 \\\\ 35\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}-0.189 \\\\ -0.340\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c2ba5073-2420-5469-9a7f-592d616a8a29" "answer-field-e8aa5d1d-a446-512d-b74d-a1d0ef0175cf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c2ba5073-2420-5469-9a7f-592d616a8a29" :answer-field/id #uuid "c2ba5073-2420-5469-9a7f-592d616a8a29" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-734cf3d0-74b6-5892-a2b6-f02c20d36503" :answer-field/choices ["answer-734cf3d0-74b6-5892-a2b6-f02c20d36503"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-734cf3d0-74b6-5892-a2b6-f02c20d36503" :answer/id #uuid "734cf3d0-74b6-5892-a2b6-f02c20d36503" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.189" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e8aa5d1d-a446-512d-b74d-a1d0ef0175cf" :answer-field/id #uuid "e8aa5d1d-a446-512d-b74d-a1d0ef0175cf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-c4500310-e39a-50db-8ce7-fa44301d2af8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c4500310-e39a-50db-8ce7-fa44301d2af8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c4500310-e39a-50db-8ce7-fa44301d2af8" :answer/id #uuid "c4500310-e39a-50db-8ce7-fa44301d2af8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.340" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204508 :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-5830491e-19ce-53b6-8fb0-b5e123e740a5" :question/id #uuid "5830491e-19ce-53b6-8fb0-b5e123e740a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-257198" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times [0,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=5{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}+2x{y}^{2}+3xy-2.\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[0,\\,1]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}+2x{y}^{2}+3xy-2) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(5{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}+2x{y}^{2}+3xy-2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10xy-4x+2{y}^{2}+3y \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+4xy+3x\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(10xy-4x+2{y}^{2}+3y) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{x}^{2}+4xy+3x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(10xy-4x+2{y}^{2}+3y) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(5{x}^{2}+4xy+3x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10y-4 & 10x+4y+3 \\\\ 10x+4y+3 & 4x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{\\begin{bmatrix}10y-4 & 10x+4y+3 \\\\ 10x+4y+3 & 4x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}10xy-4x+2{y}^{2}+3y \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+4xy+3x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with\n${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{\\begin{bmatrix}10y-4 & 10x+4y+3 \\\\ 10x+4y+3 & 4x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}10xy-4x+2{y}^{2}+3y \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+4xy+3x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}10(1)-4 & 10(0)+4(1)+3 \\\\ 10(0)+4(1)+3 & 4(0)\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}10(0)(1)-4(0)+2(1)^{2}+3(1) \\\\ 5(0)^{2}+4(0)(1)+3(0)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{6\\cdot 0-7\\cdot 7}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -7 \\\\ -7 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{49}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -35\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0.286\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e32003de-1c52-53ff-8dce-b9605e8b1759" "answer-field-3689dae0-eb0a-5015-be27-722e43abe4c1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e32003de-1c52-53ff-8dce-b9605e8b1759" :answer-field/id #uuid "e32003de-1c52-53ff-8dce-b9605e8b1759" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-358b80c0-d90b-5e9f-b42a-9bff7ca22db1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-358b80c0-d90b-5e9f-b42a-9bff7ca22db1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-358b80c0-d90b-5e9f-b42a-9bff7ca22db1" :answer/id #uuid "358b80c0-d90b-5e9f-b42a-9bff7ca22db1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3689dae0-eb0a-5015-be27-722e43abe4c1" :answer-field/id #uuid "3689dae0-eb0a-5015-be27-722e43abe4c1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6fdd4c5d-ae23-5a86-9387-8837df6a1812" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6fdd4c5d-ae23-5a86-9387-8837df6a1812"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6fdd4c5d-ae23-5a86-9387-8837df6a1812" :answer/id #uuid "6fdd4c5d-ae23-5a86-9387-8837df6a1812" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.286" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204025 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7904745e-3c48-5473-a84d-2ea698eb26ae" "question-040d983b-9569-5e20-9386-5dd2354e86bd" "question-11575fd3-3b68-5f5f-9c8d-ffa66ae7367b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-7904745e-3c48-5473-a84d-2ea698eb26ae" :question/id #uuid "7904745e-3c48-5473-a84d-2ea698eb26ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-257208" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-1,0]\\times [0,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={y}^{3}-{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}-3y.\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[-1,\\,2]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[-0.2,\\,1.2]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{2}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}-{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}-3y) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3}-{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}-3y)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2xy-4x \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-3\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2xy-4x) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-3) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2xy-4x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-3)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2y-4 & -2x \\\\ -2x & 6y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{\\begin{bmatrix}-2y-4 & -2x \\\\ -2x & 6y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}-2xy-4x \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{\\begin{bmatrix}-2y-4 & -2x \\\\ -2x & 6y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}-2xy-4x \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 \\\\ 1.2\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}-2(1.2)-4 & -2(-0.2) \\\\ -2(-0.2) & 6(1.2)\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2(-0.2)(1.2)-4(-0.2) \\\\ 3(1.2)^{2}-(-0.2)^{2}-3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 \\\\ 1.2\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}-6.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 7.2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1.28 \\\\ 1.28\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 \\\\ 1.2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{(-6.4)\\cdot 7.2-0.4\\cdot 0.4}\\begin{bmatrix}7.2 & -0.4 \\\\ -0.4 & -6.4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1.28 \\\\ 1.28\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 \\\\ 1.2\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{46.24}\\begin{bmatrix}8.704 \\\\ -8.704\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}-0.012 \\\\ 1.012\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-022dd755-e937-5f56-968f-8fdac582d253" "answer-field-940f4f61-0df6-5fbf-92d5-6c0094f915aa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-022dd755-e937-5f56-968f-8fdac582d253" :answer-field/id #uuid "022dd755-e937-5f56-968f-8fdac582d253" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-c922bd52-0c29-5fbc-bcbf-4f7cdd4dc33a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c922bd52-0c29-5fbc-bcbf-4f7cdd4dc33a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c922bd52-0c29-5fbc-b