id "q-257201" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times [1,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=4x{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-6xy-x.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $[1,\\,2]^{T}.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$10$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$-9$</td>\n<td align=\"left\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$10$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-3}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n{H}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-∇f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-6xy-x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-6xy-x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4{y}^{2}+6x-6y-1 \\\\ 8xy-6x\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{y}^{2}+6x-6y-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(8xy-6x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{y}^{2}+6x-6y-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(8xy-6x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 8y-6 \\\\ 8y-6 & 8x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 8y-6 \\\\ 8y-6 & 8x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{\\begin{bmatrix}4{y}^{2}+6x-6y-1 \\\\ 8xy-6x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 8y-6 \\\\ 8y-6 & 8x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{\\begin{bmatrix}4{y}^{2}+6x-6y-1 \\\\ 8xy-6x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 8(2)-6 \\\\ 8(2)-6 & 8(1)\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}4(2)^{2}+6(1)-6(2)-1 \\\\ 8(1)(2)-6(1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 8\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d16c318b-433e-5219-bbac-c0dceacd735f" "answer-field-65b0c455-67d2-5558-afa0-2900b8085ad6" "answer-field-002ec56f-2448-5134-85e4-8cbe3fa3a022"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d16c318b-433e-5219-bbac-c0dceacd735f" :answer-field/id #uuid "d16c318b-433e-5219-bbac-c0dceacd735f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d355a1ab-4760-5da8-81c8-ff82042eba6d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d355a1ab-4760-5da8-81c8-ff82042eba6d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d355a1ab-4760-5da8-81c8-ff82042eba6d" :answer/id #uuid "d355a1ab-4760-5da8-81c8-ff82042eba6d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-65b0c455-67d2-5558-afa0-2900b8085ad6" :answer-field/id #uuid "65b0c455-67d2-5558-afa0-2900b8085ad6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-87cb53ab-6033-5d2e-a880-f4c5d0d8a4db" :answer-field/choices ["answer-87cb53ab-6033-5d2e-a880-f4c5d0d8a4db"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87cb53ab-6033-5d2e-a880-f4c5d0d8a4db" :answer/id #uuid "87cb53ab-6033-5d2e-a880-f4c5d0d8a4db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-002ec56f-2448-5134-85e4-8cbe3fa3a022" :answer-field/id #uuid "002ec56f-2448-5134-85e4-8cbe3fa3a022" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2d1cb510-1818-547a-8900-e0080154ff41" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2d1cb510-1818-547a-8900-e0080154ff41"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d1cb510-1818-547a-8900-e0080154ff41" :answer/id #uuid "2d1cb510-1818-547a-8900-e0080154ff41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4033d90f-6820-5b6e-97e2-e268a68a992f" :question/id #uuid "4033d90f-6820-5b6e-97e2-e268a68a992f" :question/math-academy-id "q-257215" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-1,0]\\times [0,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}-xy+x-y.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $[-1,\\,1]^{T},$ and compute the components of the first iteration. Round the answer for ${x}_{1}$ to **three decimal places**.\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$-5$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$-1$\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$-5$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-3}}</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$-0.510$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-4}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n{H}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-∇f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}-xy+x-y) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}-xy+x-y)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-y+1 \\\\ -4xy-x-1\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-y+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4xy-x-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-y+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4xy-x-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6x & -4y-1 \\\\ -4y-1 & -4x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}6x & -4y-1 \\\\ -4y-1 & -4x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-y+1 \\\\ -4xy-x-1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}6x & -4y-1 \\\\ -4y-1 & -4x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-y+1 \\\\ -4xy-x-1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}6(-1) & -4(1)-1 \\\\ -4(1)-1 & -4(-1)\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}3(-1)^{2}-2(1)^{2}-1+1 \\\\ -4(-1)(1)-(-1)-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})=\\begin{bmatrix}24/49 \\\\ -19/49\\end{bmatrix}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{24}{49} \\\\ -\\frac{19}{49}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{24}{49} \\\\ -\\frac{19}{49}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}-0.510 \\\\ 0.612\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1efb643c-734b-58b8-990d-a15c076650e6" "answer-field-51ef4741-c3ce-5d22-a88d-9e3bfc963ad1" "answer-field-a8074289-ced7-55df-b16d-486ca5eaf823" "answer-field-9a9a83ee-ac72-544e-a5b3-d25e821ddf5c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1efb643c-734b-58b8-990d-a15c076650e6" :answer-field/id #uuid "1efb643c-734b-58b8-990d-a15c076650e6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-0b968de3-48ac-5509-bda5-bdd182bb0096" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0b968de3-48ac-5509-bda5-bdd182bb0096"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0b968de3-48ac-5509-bda5-bdd182bb0096" :answer/id #uuid "0b968de3-48ac-5509-bda5-bdd182bb0096" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-51ef4741-c3ce-5d22-a88d-9e3bfc963ad1" :answer-field/id #uuid "51ef4741-c3ce-5d22-a88d-9e3bfc963ad1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-c5cde4ac-83ff-571c-bb29-6811f5eb0038" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c5cde4ac-83ff-571c-bb29-6811f5eb0038"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5cde4ac-83ff-571c-bb29-6811f5eb0038" :answer/id #uuid "c5cde4ac-83ff-571c-bb29-6811f5eb0038" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a8074289-ced7-55df-b16d-486ca5eaf823" :answer-field/id #uuid "a8074289-ced7-55df-b16d-486ca5eaf823" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-60130157-f623-5097-835c-7ab98079c34b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-60130157-f623-5097-835c-7ab98079c34b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60130157-f623-5097-835c-7ab98079c34b" :answer/id #uuid "60130157-f623-5097-835c-7ab98079c34b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9a9a83ee-ac72-544e-a5b3-d25e821ddf5c" :answer-field/id #uuid "9a9a83ee-ac72-544e-a5b3-d25e821ddf5c" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-8f020a22-0c34-5fb9-b34e-9967438d2085" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8f020a22-0c34-5fb9-b34e-9967438d2085"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f020a22-0c34-5fb9-b34e-9967438d2085" :answer/id #uuid "8f020a22-0c34-5fb9-b34e-9967438d2085" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.612" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5acc736b-d0d8-5439-9126-2975fad286b2" :question/id #uuid "5acc736b-d0d8-5439-9126-2975fad286b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-257199" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,2]\\times [0,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y-4x-2y-2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $[2,\\,1]^{T}.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$4$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-3}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$-4$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$-2$\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n{H}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-∇f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}y-4x-2y-2{y}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}y-4x-2y-2{y}^{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4xy-4 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2-4y\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-2-4y) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-2-4y)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4y & 4x \\\\ 4x & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}4y & 4x \\\\ 4x & -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{\\begin{bmatrix}4xy-4 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2-4y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}4y & 4x \\\\ 4x & -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{\\begin{bmatrix}4xy-4 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2-4y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4(1) & 4(2) \\\\ 4(2) & -4\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}4(2)(1)-4 \\\\ 2(2)^{2}-2-4(1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 \\\\ 8 & -4\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a16d0722-b98a-5c1d-95f0-119e1fc538e4" "answer-field-f939bf4e-3667-591e-a976-8f0a98ca8d20" "answer-field-60d198fc-19c4-5f13-9019-6484adf07ee3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a16d0722-b98a-5c1d-95f0-119e1fc538e4" :answer-field/id #uuid "a16d0722-b98a-5c1d-95f0-119e1fc538e4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4005aa77-18e1-5073-a669-e2b096c350ec" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4005aa77-18e1-5073-a669-e2b096c350ec"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4005aa77-18e1-5073-a669-e2b096c350ec" :answer/id #uuid "4005aa77-18e1-5073-a669-e2b096c350ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f939bf4e-3667-591e-a976-8f0a98ca8d20" :answer-field/id #uuid "f939bf4e-3667-591e-a976-8f0a98ca8d20" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-a2f27189-6b5e-525a-89f5-ab63d4ef4e15" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a2f27189-6b5e-525a-89f5-ab63d4ef4e15"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2f27189-6b5e-525a-89f5-ab63d4ef4e15" :answer/id #uuid "a2f27189-6b5e-525a-89f5-ab63d4ef4e15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-60d198fc-19c4-5f13-9019-6484adf07ee3" :answer-field/id #uuid "60d198fc-19c4-5f13-9019-6484adf07ee3" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-71cba5d6-193f-5c83-907f-4c9e1d767447" :answer-field/choices ["answer-71cba5d6-193f-5c83-907f-4c9e1d767447"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71cba5d6-193f-5c83-907f-4c9e1d767447" :answer/id #uuid "71cba5d6-193f-5c83-907f-4c9e1d767447" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204160 :activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115241 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-35e39332-390d-57d5-8a71-b2e823ef2c1d" "question-0d9a8a04-d6f4-5dae-8ede-86bd3b61ce86"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-35e39332-390d-57d5-8a71-b2e823ef2c1d" :question/id #uuid "35e39332-390d-57d5-8a71-b2e823ef2c1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-131713" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x+2y &=-1 \\\\ 4x-8y &=4 \\\\ -4x+8y &=-4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 8 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 8 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 8 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 8 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now  \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$  in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2y+1 \\\\ y &=y,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2y+1 \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5be30950-b64e-577a-ae02-66dc69074ea6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5be30950-b64e-577a-ae02-66dc69074ea6" :answer-field/id #uuid "5be30950-b64e-577a-ae02-66dc69074ea6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d860981f-7d25-50fb-9bda-57a830f77a4b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-06bab61c-40c1-5534-b5a3-b01a2dbdfa45" "answer-d677f02b-fbae-5fc9-ab27-d903e38bc1fb" "answer-d860981f-7d25-50fb-9bda-57a830f77a4b" "answer-542610ce-dff1-581e-80ae-2cef01f7f353" "answer-6d65baaa-b447-55bf-9ca2-d0463511c9a4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06bab61c-40c1-5534-b5a3-b01a2dbdfa45" :answer/id #uuid "06bab61c-40c1-5534-b5a3-b01a2dbdfa45" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2y-1 \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d677f02b-fbae-5fc9-ab27-d903e38bc1fb" :answer/id #uuid "d677f02b-fbae-5fc9-ab27-d903e38bc1fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1-2y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d860981f-7d25-50fb-9bda-57a830f77a4b" :answer/id #uuid "d860981f-7d25-50fb-9bda-57a830f77a4b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2y+1 \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-542610ce-dff1-581e-80ae-2cef01f7f353" :answer/id #uuid "542610ce-dff1-581e-80ae-2cef01f7f353" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2y-1 \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d65baaa-b447-55bf-9ca2-d0463511c9a4" :answer/id #uuid "6d65baaa-b447-55bf-9ca2-d0463511c9a4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No solution" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0d9a8a04-d6f4-5dae-8ede-86bd3b61ce86" :question/id #uuid "0d9a8a04-d6f4-5dae-8ede-86bd3b61ce86" :question/math-academy-id "q-131714" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y+z &=4 \\\\ y-3z &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now  \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-5z &=2 \\\\ y-3z &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=5z+2 \\\\ y &=3z-1 \\\\ z &=z,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5z+2 \\\\ 3z-1 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-38a28058-8837-5c47-bd4f-999f7f79529b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-38a28058-8837-5c47-bd4f-999f7f79529b" :answer-field/id #uuid "38a28058-8837-5c47-bd4f-999f7f79529b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-955edd2d-0a8c-5d20-84d7-cf5a92e290b7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b6f517b4-7123-5de7-839e-f81b3c72b151" "answer-98404353-5e33-5e35-891f-c0850dff8c0f" "answer-955edd2d-0a8c-5d20-84d7-cf5a92e290b7" "answer-7cff60bc-e993-580b-aba0-33e3b2b248dd" "answer-75022ec7-7eef-58e8-96f6-520f1f5efd33"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6f517b4-7123-5de7-839e-f81b3c72b151" :answer/id #uuid "b6f517b4-7123-5de7-839e-f81b3c72b151" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2z+5 \\\\ z-3 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98404353-5e33-5e35-891f-c0850dff8c0f" :answer/id #uuid "98404353-5e33-5e35-891f-c0850dff8c0f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5z+2 \\\\ 3z+1 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-955edd2d-0a8c-5d20-84d7-cf5a92e290b7" :answer/id #uuid "955edd2d-0a8c-5d20-84d7-cf5a92e290b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5z+2 \\\\ 3z-1 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7cff60bc-e993-580b-aba0-33e3b2b248dd" :answer/id #uuid "7cff60bc-e993-580b-aba0-33e3b2b248dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5z-2 \\\\ 1-3z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75022ec7-7eef-58e8-96f6-520f1f5efd33" :answer/id #uuid "75022ec7-7eef-58e8-96f6-520f1f5efd33" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No solution" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115299 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2f4a72f8-3bd3-5788-9d12-62ca6f611891"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-2f4a72f8-3bd3-5788-9d12-62ca6f611891" :question/id #uuid "2f4a72f8-3bd3-5788-9d12-62ca6f611891" :question/math-academy-id "q-74636" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x+y &=-1 \\\\ 2x+3y &=3 \\\\ -x-4y &=-3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The system is inconsistent\n\nII. The system has a unique solution\n\nIII. The system is consistent dependent" :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final row corresponds to the equation $0x+0y=-1,$ and this has no solution. Therefore, our system has no solution, and we conclude that the system is inconsistent.\n\nAs a result, only statement I is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-eda18abf-1e9e-51ea-a1af-55bc7948bea6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-eda18abf-1e9e-51ea-a1af-55bc7948bea6" :answer-field/id #uuid "eda18abf-1e9e-51ea-a1af-55bc7948bea6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b11d6ce5-b8fc-5e47-ad56-2ad45b30686f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2a116b39-7748-5b97-8286-409d809e42cc" "answer-33822258-829b-5084-adb7-be68d8b35521" "answer-b11d6ce5-b8fc-5e47-ad56-2ad45b30686f" "answer-4eac203e-2b67-5cbd-b5f3-2f4c25980863" "answer-4ae92370-bb9c-5f64-acc7-34346d0fb34d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a116b39-7748-5b97-8286-409d809e42cc" :answer/id #uuid "2a116b39-7748-5b97-8286-409d809e42cc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33822258-829b-5084-adb7-be68d8b35521" :answer/id #uuid "33822258-829b-5084-adb7-be68d8b35521" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b11d6ce5-b8fc-5e47-ad56-2ad45b30686f" :answer/id #uuid "b11d6ce5-b8fc-5e47-ad56-2ad45b30686f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4eac203e-2b67-5cbd-b5f3-2f4c25980863" :answer/id #uuid "4eac203e-2b67-5cbd-b5f3-2f4c25980863" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ae92370-bb9c-5f64-acc7-34346d0fb34d" :answer/id #uuid "4ae92370-bb9c-5f64-acc7-34346d0fb34d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188297 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fcf32a74-971a-505b-b459-ef18eedf0a90" "question-a72cce3a-417b-593f-a3ca-475feb70703d" "question-3f8bf9ef-a3d1-5fa9-97cc-d10fddcf1cd1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-fcf32a74-971a-505b-b459-ef18eedf0a90" :question/id #uuid "fcf32a74-971a-505b-b459-ef18eedf0a90" :question/math-academy-id "q-143715" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*" :question/worked-solution "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta ){cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta ){cos}^{\\cancel{2}}⁡(\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{0-2\\cdot 1}{-3\\cdot 1\\cdot 0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{0} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0,$ there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](images/f4/f4609e6433a861ef255cbeeac60c8a316e5c14a89df45030e26e526e3a1253a4.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e16e2010-e951-50c2-8dc9-1ddcbae83f31"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e16e2010-e951-50c2-8dc9-1ddcbae83f31" :answer-field/id #uuid "e16e2010-e951-50c2-8dc9-1ddcbae83f31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e136fdfa-26d6-5d9d-8c09-dcdc748b9749" :answer-field/choices ["answer-313ec59c-5153-5bc7-8ccf-d7d49fff1f8e" "answer-b3ef2881-d27a-5bf5-a98e-41fea5f9b27b" "answer-e136fdfa-26d6-5d9d-8c09-dcdc748b9749" "answer-70b0c1ec-d41a-509f-b845-f8b330322fdc" "answer-5ffa960b-f359-54f3-bfe0-2df8dcedbd09"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-313ec59c-5153-5bc7-8ccf-d7d49fff1f8e" :answer/id #uuid "313ec59c-5153-5bc7-8ccf-d7d49fff1f8e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3ef2881-d27a-5bf5-a98e-41fea5f9b27b" :answer/id #uuid "b3ef2881-d27a-5bf5-a98e-41fea5f9b27b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e136fdfa-26d6-5d9d-8c09-dcdc748b9749" :answer/id #uuid "e136fdfa-26d6-5d9d-8c09-dcdc748b9749" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70b0c1ec-d41a-509f-b845-f8b330322fdc" :answer/id #uuid "70b0c1ec-d41a-509f-b845-f8b330322fdc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ffa960b-f359-54f3-bfe0-2df8dcedbd09" :answer/id #uuid "5ffa960b-f359-54f3-bfe0-2df8dcedbd09" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a72cce3a-417b-593f-a3ca-475feb70703d" :question/id #uuid "a72cce3a-417b-593f-a3ca-475feb70703d" :question/math-academy-id "q-144906" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=3{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is undefined\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*" :question/worked-solution "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =3{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{3{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{12{sin}^{2}⁡(\\theta ){cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{12{sin}^{\\cancel{2}}⁡(\\theta ){\\cancel{cos}}^{2}(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{8\\cancel{sin}\\theta {cos}^{\\cancel{3}}⁡\\theta \\,(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})}{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})(3{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}{0\\cdot (3\\cdot 0-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-12}{0} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0,$ there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](images/32/32a586a1fe160a6c3a2cfee3cfb78af3d596679cf40abedeb0b8d622a3cf736c.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4b4c2f95-49e5-5e7b-9fbf-f50c23d0825b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4b4c2f95-49e5-5e7b-9fbf-f50c23d0825b" :answer-field/id #uuid "4b4c2f95-49e5-5e7b-9fbf-f50c23d0825b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-742dc51a-8d96-560e-94d1-367169dd0392" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fb85d25b-c824-57bd-a2e9-670821d95958" "answer-e1476574-57fd-5884-b17f-d8c6e1916591" "answer-c42187b6-4c29-5b59-b6f3-624ff226be39" "answer-d8c3238c-1f70-547d-9e2f-c99ec90befda" "answer-742dc51a-8d96-560e-94d1-367169dd0392"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb85d25b-c824-57bd-a2e9-670821d95958" :answer/id #uuid "fb85d25b-c824-57bd-a2e9-670821d95958" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1476574-57fd-5884-b17f-d8c6e1916591" :answer/id #uuid "e1476574-57fd-5884-b17f-d8c6e1916591" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c42187b6-4c29-5b59-b6f3-624ff226be39" :answer/id #uuid "c42187b6-4c29-5b59-b6f3-624ff226be39" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8c3238c-1f70-547d-9e2f-c99ec90befda" :answer/id #uuid "d8c3238c-1f70-547d-9e2f-c99ec90befda" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-742dc51a-8d96-560e-94d1-367169dd0392" :answer/id #uuid "742dc51a-8d96-560e-94d1-367169dd0392" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3f8bf9ef-a3d1-5fa9-97cc-d10fddcf1cd1" :question/id #uuid "3f8bf9ef-a3d1-5fa9-97cc-d10fddcf1cd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-144911" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )={cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta (5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*" :question/worked-solution "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}){cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})(5{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-3) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0\\cdot (5\\cdot 0-3) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3)}{{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\cancel{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3)}{{cos}^{\\cancel{3}}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (5\\cdot 0-3)}{0\\cdot (5\\cdot 0-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{0} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0,$ there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](images/d7/d7881ba860bcc32301a162eb200ea9075b06b63c780be74c14aedbcb1654eaaa.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ca41ebba-af00-5d55-bf09-af4bba36416b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ca41ebba-af00-5d55-bf09-af4bba36416b" :answer-field/id #uuid "ca41ebba-af00-5d55-bf09-af4bba36416b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4ede0597-e201-5e81-953f-0df38ea579f3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0fe0a563-e910-5a09-9b65-423c48c76310" "answer-4ede0597-e201-5e81-953f-0df38ea579f3" "answer-a2b00882-095d-51f2-92e9-ed2560cdc8b6" "answer-7a918a37-015b-582f-9559-4c91adb530ad" "answer-e7f0df0d-c797-5e92-9ea6-b642a07c5dd9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0fe0a563-e910-5a09-9b65-423c48c76310" :answer/id #uuid "0fe0a563-e910-5a09-9b65-423c48c76310" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ede0597-e201-5e81-953f-0df38ea579f3" :answer/id #uuid "4ede0597-e201-5e81-953f-0df38ea579f3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2b00882-095d-51f2-92e9-ed2560cdc8b6" :answer/id #uuid "a2b00882-095d-51f2-92e9-ed2560cdc8b6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a918a37-015b-582f-9559-4c91adb530ad" :answer/id #uuid "7a918a37-015b-582f-9559-4c91adb530ad" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e7f0df0d-c797-5e92-9ea6-b642a07c5dd9" :answer/id #uuid "e7f0df0d-c797-5e92-9ea6-b642a07c5dd9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188316 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1355c292-a399-578d-8bac-2939b3befda0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-1355c292-a399-578d-8bac-2939b3befda0" :question/id #uuid "1355c292-a399-578d-8bac-2939b3befda0" :question/math-academy-id "q-145039" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\infty$\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" :question/worked-solution "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta ={\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty \\,\\text{and}\\,{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${x}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}){cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to {(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{+},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{\\cancel{cos}\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{\\cancel{2}}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{0-2\\cdot 1}{-3\\cdot 1\\cdot {0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using the calculations from part II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{0-2\\cdot 1}{-3\\cdot 1\\cdot {0}^{+}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/50/50637a502a56fc242ba5d8e2fda59ddc899295d4b14f7ae692869536b95ef661.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fce87f54-6287-5426-bb0b-5253c4ba2656"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fce87f54-6287-5426-bb0b-5253c4ba2656" :answer-field/id #uuid "fce87f54-6287-5426-bb0b-5253c4ba2656" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-846fb0c9-3ec1-54f5-b33c-0e023a6c3914" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c63e937b-281c-584f-875d-739cf8a9c9da" "answer-f0214696-0b2a-5443-87e4-6a0873c5d70e" "answer-846fb0c9-3ec1-54f5-b33c-0e023a6c3914" "answer-864177d3-700a-5680-a7ae-fe5e85113f8a" "answer-70f01508-769b-5ace-9917-5f7722cd7ce8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c63e937b-281c-584f-875d-739cf8a9c9da" :answer/id #uuid "c63e937b-281c-584f-875d-739cf8a9c9da" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f0214696-0b2a-5443-87e4-6a0873c5d70e" :answer/id #uuid "f0214696-0b2a-5443-87e4-6a0873c5d70e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-846fb0c9-3ec1-54f5-b33c-0e023a6c3914" :answer/id #uuid "846fb0c9-3ec1-54f5-b33c-0e023a6c3914" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-864177d3-700a-5680-a7ae-fe5e85113f8a" :answer/id #uuid "864177d3-700a-5680-a7ae-fe5e85113f8a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70f01508-769b-5ace-9917-5f7722cd7ce8" :answer/id #uuid "70f01508-769b-5ace-9917-5f7722cd7ce8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188613 :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-3c665c19-95e5-52b1-a4a9-44e5ed397b25" :activity/id #uuid "3c665c19-95e5-52b1-a4a9-44e5ed397b25" :activity/title "Horizontal and Vertical Tangents to Polar Curves in Non-Differentiable Cases" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049521 :activity/course 17592186046673 :activity/steps ["step-48a6f89e-446f-5ff8-9fe2-e64eff3be6f6" "step-5e976fb1-d35b-5c20-ade1-2919ee77f71b" "step-63ba05e5-32e7-552d-a5e3-1d3bf124648b" "step-4b0acf10-61d4-57d2-8625-d0ac303e7832" "step-4222932c-dad4-5f43-9169-5224c25483a0"] :activity/first-step "step-48a6f89e-446f-5ff8-9fe2-e64eff3be6f6" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-48a6f89e-446f-5ff8-9fe2-e64eff3be6f6" :step/id #uuid "48a6f89e-446f-5ff8-9fe2-e64eff3be6f6" :step/content "question-fcf32a74-971a-505b-b459-ef18eedf0a90" :step/next "step-5e976fb1-d35b-5c20-ade1-2919ee77f71b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-5e976fb1-d35b-5c20-ade1-2919ee77f71b" :step/id #uuid "5e976fb1-d35b-5c20-ade1-2919ee77f71b" :step/content "question-a72cce3a-417b-593f-a3ca-475feb70703d" :step/next "step-63ba05e5-32e7-552d-a5e3-1d3bf124648b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-63ba05e5-32e7-552d-a5e3-1d3bf124648b" :step/id #uuid "63ba05e5-32e7-552d-a5e3-1d3bf124648b" :step/content "question-3f8bf9ef-a3d1-5fa9-97cc-d10fddcf1cd1" :step/next "step-4b0acf10-61d4-57d2-8625-d0ac303e7832" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-4b0acf10-61d4-57d2-8625-d0ac303e7832" :step/id #uuid "4b0acf10-61d4-57d2-8625-d0ac303e7832" :step/content "question-1355c292-a399-578d-8bac-2939b3befda0" :step/next "step-4222932c-dad4-5f43-9169-5224c25483a0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-4222932c-dad4-5f43-9169-5224c25483a0" :step/id #uuid "4222932c-dad4-5f43-9169-5224c25483a0" :step/content 17592186188613 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115330 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-586758ad-b0a5-531e-b46a-bdc8f3f5617e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-586758ad-b0a5-531e-b46a-bdc8f3f5617e" :question/id #uuid "586758ad-b0a5-531e-b46a-bdc8f3f5617e" :question/math-academy-id "q-93034" :question/problem "Find the domain of $h(x)=\\sqrt[3]{-x-5}+7.$" :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is\n$x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTransformations do not change this interval. Therefore, the domain of $h(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bdfbe9cc-d10f-546a-ae0e-e1474b7f55ea"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bdfbe9cc-d10f-546a-ae0e-e1474b7f55ea" :answer-field/id #uuid "bdfbe9cc-d10f-546a-ae0e-e1474b7f55ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-32dff668-6e85-5887-aa82-74cffacd267a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b36b6cd7-06da-5a52-9dcc-6f8d57cdbbbd" "answer-1a3e85fe-2334-5a38-931b-c6c141d8347d" "answer-1fc7d446-c329-5e31-8f6c-0aaacbc905ed" "answer-32dff668-6e85-5887-aa82-74cffacd267a" "answer-5e3f8063-098a-51d0-abac-40f0c38c4085"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b36b6cd7-06da-5a52-9dcc-6f8d57cdbbbd" :answer/id #uuid "b36b6cd7-06da-5a52-9dcc-6f8d57cdbbbd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-5,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a3e85fe-2334-5a38-931b-c6c141d8347d" :answer/id #uuid "1a3e85fe-2334-5a38-931b-c6c141d8347d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1fc7d446-c329-5e31-8f6c-0aaacbc905ed" :answer/id #uuid "1fc7d446-c329-5e31-8f6c-0aaacbc905ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-32dff668-6e85-5887-aa82-74cffacd267a" :answer/id #uuid "32dff668-6e85-5887-aa82-74cffacd267a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e3f8063-098a-51d0-abac-40f0c38c4085" :answer/id #uuid "5e3f8063-098a-51d0-abac-40f0c38c4085" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (0,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115314 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-54e659be-f31e-5f00-888e-77687d0dffe4" "question-7823b972-423e-5466-806b-703ca14c80f8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-54e659be-f31e-5f00-888e-77687d0dffe4" :question/id #uuid "54e659be-f31e-5f00-888e-77687d0dffe4" :question/math-academy-id "q-93048" :question/problem "Find the domain of $q(x)=2\\sqrt[4]{x+3}-2.$" :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\sqrt[4]{x}$ is\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $q(x)=2\\sqrt[4]{x+3}-2,$ we replace $x$ with $x+3$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\nx+3\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge -3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=q(x)$ is $x\\in [-3,\\infty ).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=q(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/97/977b12dd8bd88d2b79285ef07d89f9958194fc84f340d7930c5b40122f8896d2.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-229676b2-2ee0-5238-ae2a-c792f196f8eb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-229676b2-2ee0-5238-ae2a-c792f196f8eb" :answer-field/id #uuid "229676b2-2ee0-5238-ae2a-c792f196f8eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-45eb3e1e-5bf8-5fa5-8798-ad8e5416e175" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c4a84016-a105-56d3-be93-bb62a2c31e28" "answer-2a96d10d-da6d-5545-aa04-dba500589752" "answer-29de12d5-e988-519a-b0a4-78f0306fc661" "answer-45eb3e1e-5bf8-5fa5-8798-ad8e5416e175" "answer-0bec1894-d269-5eae-a947-8368616dd1ed"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c4a84016-a105-56d3-be93-bb62a2c31e28" :answer/id #uuid "c4a84016-a105-56d3-be93-bb62a2c31e28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (\\infty ,-3]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a96d10d-da6d-5545-aa04-dba500589752" :answer/id #uuid "2a96d10d-da6d-5545-aa04-dba500589752" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (\\infty ,3]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29de12d5-e988-519a-b0a4-78f0306fc661" :answer/id #uuid "29de12d5-e988-519a-b0a4-78f0306fc661" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in [-3,3]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45eb3e1e-5bf8-5fa5-8798-ad8e5416e175" :answer/id #uuid "45eb3e1e-5bf8-5fa5-8798-ad8e5416e175" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in [-3,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0bec1894-d269-5eae-a947-8368616dd1ed" :answer/id #uuid "0bec1894-d269-5eae-a947-8368616dd1ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in [3,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7823b972-423e-5466-806b-703ca14c80f8" :question/id #uuid "7823b972-423e-5466-806b-703ca14c80f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-98631" :question/problem "Find the domain of $s(x)=\\sqrt[6]{2x-4}+1.$" :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\sqrt[6]{x}$ is\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $s(x)=\\sqrt[6]{2x-4}+1,$ we replace $x$ with $2x-4$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n2x-4\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 2x & \\ge 4 \\\\ x & \\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=s(x)$ is $x\\in [2,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=s(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c340fbe03170ab0dcd2ba0a7c7c32ed6712f96b0eaa944cde2dc40a232e3e32e.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c0e0985b-75bc-5d5f-8b91-eb1bc0518ee9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c0e0985b-75bc-5d5f-8b91-eb1bc0518ee9" :answer-field/id #uuid "c0e0985b-75bc-5d5f-8b91-eb1bc0518ee9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-037fd035-ab0e-5bed-9f2a-d0934fa82574" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6fc57805-d59e-554a-a8df-6378c378a54d" "answer-9f27a8ae-d48c-5273-8f21-4484bcd3f3